Package: walkbench
Type: Package
Title: Testbed of Walking Models for the Divergence Exponents of Gait
Version: 0.6.2
Authors@R:
    person("Philippe", "Terrier", , "philippe.terrier@he-arc.ch",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: Walking models share a common interface, so that an experiment
    written for one model runs on the others without change. The interface
    has a perturbation port that accepts any input series, a closed-loop port
    for corrective controllers and a metronome, and a linearization at the
    fixed point. Runs have standardized outputs, and a run driver applies a
    seed rule, redraws a run after a fall and writes a run log. Common
    estimators compute the short-term and long-term divergence exponents
    with the Rosenstein engine of RACING v1.0.2 (Terrier 2026)
    <doi:10.5281/zenodo.20542521>, the Floquet multipliers, the kinematic
    variability and the scaling exponent of detrended fluctuation analysis.
    The package contains two models. M1 is the simplest walking model of
    Garcia et al. (1998) <doi:10.1115/1.2798313>, perturbed at each foot
    strike as in Su and Dingwell (2007) <doi:10.1115/1.2800760>. M2 is the
    walking model with arced feet and a hip spring of Hobbelen and Wisse
    (2007) <doi:10.1109/TRO.2007.904908>, as used by Bruijn et al. (2012)
    <doi:10.1016/j.medengphy.2011.07.024>. Its input is the height of the
    floor at each step. The campaign rules follow the analysis plan of the
    SIM-ACI project, registered on OSF Registries <doi:10.17605/OSF.IO/M74TK>.
    They cover the master seeds, the transients, the rescaling of the inputs,
    the real stride series and their surrogates, and the fall rate of each
    cell. An analytic predictor gives the expected divergence curve and the
    long-term exponent from the linearization of a model and the
    autocovariance of its input.
